// XFAIL: RT-485: `fn kf<T>(x: T) -> &'static i64 where &'static i64: From<T> { From::from(x) }` is refused in its body, borrowck.outlives "the lifetime in 'T' must outlive 'static": the where clause already says it; rustc builds it and `Some(&K).map(kf::<&'static i64>)` prints `Some(1000)` (0.30.2) [0.30.2: the body builds (rust_b_fn_value_static_where_direct_ok); used as a value it is refused, borrowck.fn_value_static, by D4.7 as `fn ks(x: &'static i64)` is since v0.30.1 (a call through a fn value or an Fn bound gives a reference of any lifetime); the release built it before static_param read where clauses only through the hole that also built `v.first().map(kf::<&i64>)` as a use after free (rust_b_err_fn_value_static_where_uaf); open: D4.7 to require 'static of the bound's argument instead]
// From the re-review of the fn-value work (g302fnval). Since v0.30.1 at least.
// The review's sp01 passes a Vec's element instead of a static's, which
// rustc refuses at the call (E0597); vole refuses kf itself.
// EXPECT: Some(1000)
static K: i64 = 1000;

fn kf<T>(x: T) -> &'static i64
    where
        & 'static i64: From<T>,
{
    From::from(x)
}

fn main() {
    let a = Some(&K).map(kf::<&'static i64>);
    println("{:?}", a);
}
